  up small.

  First question, and it's the one that constrains everything downstream. "Persist" has two very different meanings
  here, and they differ in what you can build on later:

  - [1mIn-memory for the page's lifetime[0m — the id lives in a module variable, gone on refresh or a new tab. Buys a trivial,
    dependency-free implementation with no security surface. Costs: every reload loses who logged in, so any multi-page
    flow breaks. Cheap to upgrade to real storage later if the read/write goes through one module.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reloads and in-tab navigation, dies when the tab closes, and is not shared across tabs.
    Buys working multi-page flows with a lifetime that roughly matches "a login session." Costs: the id becomes readable
    by any script on the page.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives reloads, tab close, and browser restart, shared across tabs. Buys the longest-lived
    identity. Costs the same script-readability, plus the id sticks around indefinitely until something explicitly
    clears it, which is a real consideration for anything identifying a user.

  The storage choice is a stored format, so it's the expensive one to change later — the other two options are mostly a
  matter of swapping the module's internals if everything reads through one place.

  My recommendation is [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m: it actually satisfies "persist so the rest of the app can tell who logged in"
  across the page loads other forms would live on, without leaving a user identifier on the machine after the browser
  closes. I'd only go to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m if you want the id to outlive the browser session, and that's a decision I'd want
  you to make deliberately rather than inherit.
